cells:
  - kind: 1
    value: "### Historical sales opportunity performance analysis\r

      \r

      #### **Project overview**\r

      <small>\r

      You are a business analyst supporting the sales leadership team of a
      fictional B2B technology company. Leadership wants to better understand
      historical sales performance across completed opportunities.\r

      \r

      Your task is to use the company’s Oracle database to investigate where
      sales outcomes differ across customers, opportunity types, sales
      organization, and products. You will communicate findings and recommend
      actions that sales leadership should investigate, monitor, or improve.\r

      \r

      This project uses descriptive and diagnostic analytics only. You will not
      build predictive models, use logistic regression, score opportunities, or
      recommend which individual live opportunities to prioritize.\r

      \r

      <b>Business scenario</b><br>\r

      Sales leadership has many completed sales opportunities in its CRM system.
      Management wants to understand:\r

      - Where did historical sales performance differ, and what should sales
      leadership investigate, monitor, or improve?\r

      \r

      Your analysis should help leadership understand historical patterns in win
      rates, completed sales-cycle duration, product mix, and sales activity.
      Your conclusions must be supported by evidence from the database.\r

      \r

      A finding of “no meaningful difference” is a valid result when supported
      by your analysis.\r

      </small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT\r

      \    a.INDUSTRY,\r

      \    COUNT(*) AS total_opportunities,\r

      \    SUM(CASE WHEN o.STAGE = 'Closed Won' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS
      won_opportunities,\r

      \    SUM(CASE WHEN o.STAGE = 'Closed Lost' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS
      lost_opportunities,\r

      \    ROUND(\r

      \        SUM(CASE WHEN o.STAGE = 'Closed Won' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) * 100.0 /
      COUNT(*),\r

      \        1\r

      \    ) AS win_rate_pct\r

      FROM DATABASE_FUNDAMENTALS.OPPORTUNITY o\r

      JOIN DATABASE_FUNDAMENTALS.ACCOUNT a\r

      \    ON o.ACCOUNT_ID = a.ACCOUNT_ID\r

      GROUP BY a.INDUSTRY\r

      ORDER BY win_rate_pct DESC;\r

      \r

      \r

      \r

      \r

      \r

      \r

      \r

      \r

      \r

      \r

      \r

      \r

      \r

      \r

      -- ============================================\r

      -- 1. Row count for OPPORTUNITY\r

      -- ============================================\r

      SELECT COUNT(*) AS opportunity_rows FROM
      DATABASE_FUNDAMENTALS.OPPORTUNITY;\r

      \r

      \r

      -- ============================================\r

      -- 2. Row count for ACCOUNT\r

      -- ============================================\r

      SELECT COUNT(*) AS account_rows FROM DATABASE_FUNDAMENTALS.ACCOUNT;\r

      \r

      \r

      -- ============================================\r

      -- 3. Duplicate check: OPPORTUNITY_ID\r

      -- (If this returns 0 rows, there are NO duplicates)\r

      -- ============================================\r

      SELECT OPPORTUNITY_ID, COUNT(*)\r

      FROM DATABASE_FUNDAMENTALS.OPPORTUNITY\r

      GROUP BY OPPORTUNITY_ID\r

      HAVING COUNT(*) > 1;\r

      \r

      \r

      -- ============================================\r

      -- 4. Duplicate check: ACCOUNT_ID\r

      -- (If this returns 0 rows, there are NO duplicates)\r

      -- ============================================\r

      SELECT ACCOUNT_ID, COUNT(*)\r

      FROM DATABASE_FUNDAMENTALS.ACCOUNT\r

      GROUP BY ACCOUNT_ID\r

      HAVING COUNT(*) > 1;\r

      \r

      \r

      -- ============================================\r

      -- 5. Null counts for OPPORTUNITY columns that allow nulls\r

      -- (CLOSED_WON_REASON, PUSH_COUNT, AGE)\r

      -- ============================================\r

      SELECT\r

      \    SUM(CASE WHEN CLOSED_WON_REASON IS NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS
      null_closed_won_reason,\r

      \    SUM(CASE WHEN PUSH_COUNT IS NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS
      null_push_count,\r

      \    SUM(CASE WHEN AGE IS NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS null_age\r

      FROM DATABASE_FUNDAMENTALS.OPPORTUNITY;\r

      \r

      \r

      -- ============================================\r

      -- 6. Null counts for ACCOUNT columns used in your analysis\r

      -- (INDUSTRY is the one you actually use for Q1)\r

      -- ============================================\r

      SELECT\r

      \    SUM(CASE WHEN INDUSTRY IS NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS null_industry,\r

      \    SUM(CASE WHEN ANNUAL_REVENUE IS NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS
      null_annual_revenue,\r

      \    SUM(CASE WHEN REGION IS NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS null_region,\r

      \    SUM(CASE WHEN COUNTRY IS NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS null_country\r

      FROM DATABASE_FUNDAMENTALS.ACCOUNT;\r

      \r

      \r

      -- ============================================\r

      -- 7. Date range for OPPORTUNITY\r

      -- ============================================\r

      SELECT\r

      \    MIN(CREATED_DATE) AS earliest_created,\r

      \    MAX(CREATED_DATE) AS latest_created,\r

      \    MIN(CLOSED_DATE) AS earliest_closed,\r

      \    MAX(CLOSED_DATE) AS latest_closed\r

      FROM DATABASE_FUNDAMENTALS.OPPORTUNITY;\r\n"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "#### **S - Specify the question**\r

      <small>\r

      <b>Primary business question</b><br>\r

      Among completed opportunities, where did historical win rate, sales-cycle
      duration, and product mix differ, and what should sales leadership
      investigate, monitor, or improve?\r

      \r

      \r

      You must also develop two supporting analytical questions.\r

      \r

      Examples include:\r

      - How do win rates differ by customer industry, customer geography, or
      account revenue band?\r

      - How do outcomes differ by opportunity type, business unit, sales team,
      or owner?\r

      - How does completed sales-cycle duration differ between won and lost
      opportunities?\r

      - How do push counts differ between won and lost opportunities?\r

      - Which products appear most often in won and lost opportunities?\r

      - Do opportunities containing multiple products differ from single-product
      opportunities?\r

      \r

      You may develop other supporting questions if they are answerable with the
      available data. Your analysis may identify associations or differences
      between groups. Do not claim that a factor caused an outcome unless the
      data and method support a causal conclusion.\r

      <small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "<small>\r

      <b>Analytical question #1</b><br>\r

      <b>Question:</b> How does historical win rate differ by customer
      industry?<br>\r

      <b>Relevant KPI or comparison:</b> Win rate (won opportunities ÷ total
      completed opportunities), grouped by customer industry, with the
      underlying opportunity count reported alongside each rate.<br>\r

      <b>Relevance:</b> Identifying industries with higher or lower historical
      win rates helps leadership understand where the company closes deals more
      effectively and where sales messaging, qualification, or resourcing may
      need attention.<br>\r

      <b>Tableau workbook sheet name:</b> Win Rate by Industry\r

      </small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "/*\r

      Analytical question # 1 SQL\r

      This should be a SQL query that returns the data\r

      necessary to answer the analytical question.\r

      You'll need to create a visualization based on the query results\r

      that effectively communicates the insights derived from the data.\r

      */\r

      -- Place your query below this line.\r

      \r

      SELECT \r

      \    a.INDUSTRY,\r

      \    COUNT(*) AS total_opportunities,\r

      \    SUM(CASE WHEN o.STAGE = 'Closed Won' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS
      won_opportunities,\r

      \    SUM(CASE WHEN o.STAGE = 'Closed Lost' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS
      lost_opportunities,\r

      \    ROUND(\r

      \        SUM(CASE WHEN o.STAGE = 'Closed Won' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) * 100.0 /
      COUNT(*),\r

      \        1\r

      \    ) AS win_rate_pct\r

      FROM DATABASE_FUNDAMENTALS.OPPORTUNITY o\r

      JOIN DATABASE_FUNDAMENTALS.ACCOUNT a\r

      \    ON o.ACCOUNT_ID = a.ACCOUNT_ID\r

      GROUP BY a.INDUSTRY\r

      ORDER BY win_rate_pct DESC;\r\n"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "<small>\r

      <b>Analytical question #1</b><br>\r

      <b>Question:</b> [Your analytical question.]<br>\r

      <b>Relevant KPI or comparison:</b> [State the metric or comparison that
      will answer this question.]<br>\r

      <b>Relevance:</b> [How the analytical question is relevant to the primary
      business question.]<br>\r

      <b>Tableau workbook sheet name:</b> [Insert the Tableau workbook sheet
      name associated with exploring and answering this analytical.]\r

      </small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "/*\r

      Analytical question # 2 SQL\r

      This should be a SQL query that returns the data\r

      necessary to answer the analytical question.\r

      You'll need to create a visualization based on the query results\r

      that effectively communicates the insights derived from the data.\r

      */\r

      -- Place your query below this line.\r\n"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "<small>\r

      <b>Anticipated data limitations relevant to your analytical
      questions</b><br>\r

      [Anticipated data limitations]\r

      <small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "#### **O - Obtain the data**\r

      \r

      <small>\r

      <b>Data source</b><br>\r

      You will work with the fictional Salesforce-style data in the
      DATABASE_FUNDAMENTALS Oracle schema.<br>\r

      \r

      <b>Relevant tables may include:</b>\r

      \r

      <table>\r

      \  <thead>\r

      \    <tr>\r

      \      <th>Table</th>\r

      \      <th>General content</th>\r

      \    </tr>\r

      \  </thead>\r

      \  <tbody>\r

      \    <tr>\r

      \      <td>OPPORTUNITY</td>\r

      \      <td>Historical sales opportunities and final outcomes</td>\r

      \    </tr>\r

      \    <tr>\r

      \      <td>OPPORTUNITY_LINE_ITEM</td>\r

      \      <td>Products and quantities associated with opportunities</td>\r

      \    </tr>\r

      \    <tr>\r

      \      <td>ACCOUNT</td>\r

      \      <td>Customer/account characteristics</td>\r

      \    </tr>\r

      \    <tr>\r

      \      <td>OWNER</td>\r

      \      <td>Sales-owner characteristics</td>\r

      \    </tr>\r

      \    <tr>\r

      \      <td>PRODUCT</td>\r

      \      <td>Product names and catalog prices</td>\r

      \    </tr>\r

      \    <tr>\r

      \      <td>BUSINESS_UNIT</td>\r

      \      <td>Sales business units</td>\r

      \    </tr>\r

      \    <tr>\r

      \      <td>SALES_TEAM</td>\r

      \      <td>Sales-team hierarchy</td>\r

      \    </tr>\r

      \  </tbody>\r

      </table><br>\r

      You do not need to use every table. Select the tables and fields needed to
      answer your questions.\r

      <b>\r

      It is critical that you understand the grain of each table and validate
      that your JOINs do not unintentionally change the meaning of your measures
      or cause them to return the incorrect results.\r

      </b>\r

      </small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "<small>\r

      <b>Evidence of proper exploration and understanding of tables used for
      analysis</b>\r

      </small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "<small>\r

      <b>Fully qualified table 1 name:</b>
      DATABASE_FUNDAMENTALS.OPPORTUNITY<br>\r

      <b>Grain:</b> Each row represents a single historical sales opportunity
      and its final outcome.<br>\r

      <b>Primary key:</b> OPPORTUNITY_ID<br>\r

      <b>Foreign key(s):</b> ACCOUNT_ID, OWNER_ID<br>\r

      <b>Relationship(s):</b> Many-to-one relationship with the ACCOUNT
      table.<br>\r

      <b>Number of rows:</b> 500,000<br>\r

      <b>Number of duplicates:</b> 0<br>\r

      <b>Number of nulls:</b> CLOSED_WON_REASON: 0, PUSH_COUNT: 0, AGE: 0<br>\r

      <b>Date-range:</b> Min created: 10-FEB-21, Max created: 07-JAN-26; Min
      closed: 09-FEB-23, Max closed: 07-FEB-26\r

      \r

      <br><br>\r

      \r

      <b>Fully qualified table 2 name:</b> DATABASE_FUNDAMENTALS.ACCOUNT<br>\r

      <b>Grain:</b> Each row represents a single customer account and its core
      characteristics.<br>\r

      <b>Primary key:</b> ACCOUNT_ID<br>\r

      <b>Foreign key(s):</b> Not applicable<br>\r

      <b>Relationship(s):</b> One-to-many relationship with the OPPORTUNITY
      table.<br>\r

      <b>Number of rows:</b> 110<br>\r

      <b>Number of duplicates:</b> 0<br>\r

      <b>Number of nulls:</b> INDUSTRY: 0, ANNUAL_REVENUE: 0, REGION: 0,
      COUNTRY: 0<br>\r

      <b>Date-range:</b> Not applicable\r

      </small>\r

      \r

      <b>Fully qualified table 1 name</b><br>\r

      <b>Grain:</b> [How the KPI is calculated in plain-English.]<br>\r

      <b>Primary key:</b> [The primary key of the table.]<br>\r

      <b>Foreign key(s):</b> [The fields serving as foreign keys within the
      table, if applicable. Populate this section with \"Not applicable\" if no
