      \r

      <b>Fully qualified table 1 name</b><br>\r

      <b>Grain:</b> [How the KPI is calculated in plain-English.]<br>\r

      <b>Primary key:</b> [The primary key of the table.]<br>\r

      <b>Foreign key(s):</b> [The fields serving as foreign keys within the
      table, if applicable. Populate this section with \"Not applicable\" if no
      foreign keys exist within the table.]<br>\r

      <b>Relationship(s):</b> [How the KPI is calculated in
      plain-English.]<br>\r

      <b>Number of rows:</b> [The number of rows within the table.]<br>\r

      <b>Number of duplicates:</b> [The number of duplicates identified within
      the table.]<br>\r

      <b>Number of nulls:</b> [The number of nulls for each field used for your
      analysis.]<br>\r

      <b>Date-range:</b> [The minimum and maximum date of data available within
      a table. If a table does not contain a date field, populate with \"Not
      applicable.\"]\r

      </small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "<small>\r

      <b>Data limitations</b><br>\r

      Keep these limitations in mind:\r

      <ul>\r

      \  <li>All opportunities in the supplied data are already closed.</li>\r

      \  <li>CLOSED_WON_REASON is recorded after the outcome and must not be
      used as a driver of success.</li>\r

      \  <li>AGE represents final completed sales-cycle duration and may be used
      only for historical comparisons.</li>\r

      \  <li>Product price and quantity do not establish recognized revenue,
      transaction revenue, discounts, or currency-adjusted revenue.</li>\r

      \  <li>Product prices are denominated in USD. For this project, calculate
      line-item revenue as quantity multiplied by product price. Calculate
      opportunity revenue as the sum of line-item revenue for that
      opportunity.</li>\r

      \  <li>Account region and owner region represent different concepts:
      customer geography and sales coverage geography.</li>\r

      \  <li>Some calendar years contain partial data. Use comparable time
      periods when making time-based comparisons.</li>\r

      </ul>\r

      </small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "#### **A - Analyze the data**\r

      <small>\r

      <p><b>Required baseline analysis</b><br>All students must calculate via
      SQL:</p>\r

      <ul>\r

      \  <li>Total completed opportunities</li>\r

      \  <li>Number of won opportunities</li>\r

      \  <li>Number of lost opportunities</li>\r

      \  <li>Historical win rate</li>\r

      \  <li>Median completed sales-cycle duration</li>\r

      \  <li>Median completed sales-cycle duration by outcome</li>\r

      \  <li>Opportunity volume and win rate for at least one business
      dimension</li>\r

      \  <li>Product mix and either average line-item count or average number of
      distinct products per opportunity. Product-mix guidance: Product mix
      refers to the individual products included in completed opportunities.
      Analyze product presence, quantity, and the number of distinct products or
      opportunity line items per opportunity. Because an opportunity may contain
      multiple products, product-presence groups overlap and should not be added
      together as mutually exclusive categories.</li>\r

      \  <li>Total opportunity revenue, won revenue and lost revenue</li>\r

      \  <li>Average won opportunity value</li>\r

      </ul>\r

      <p>Report the count alongside every rate. Do not treat a small difference
      in a rate as meaningful without considering the underlying opportunity
      count and practical business relevance.</p>\r

      </small>"
    languageId: markdown
