  - kind: 1
    value: "<small>\r

      <p><b>Diagnostic analysis</b><br>\r

      Select <b>two analytical themes</b> and create <b>three</b> diagnostic
      visualizations.</b></p>\r

      <p><b>Possible themes include:</b></p>\r

      <table>\r

      \  <thead>\r

      \    <tr>\r

      \      <th>Theme</th>\r

      \      <th>Example dimensions</th>\r

      \    </tr>\r

      \  </thead>\r

      \  <tbody>\r

      \    <tr>\r

      \      <td>Customer/account</td>\r

      \      <td>Industry, account annual-revenue band, customer region,
      country</td>\r

      \    </tr>\r

      \    <tr>\r

      \      <td>Opportunity</td>\r

      \      <td>Opportunity type, completed sales-cycle duration, push
      count</td>\r

      \    </tr>\r

      \    <tr>\r

      \      <td>Sales organization</td>\r

      \      <td>Owner, team, business unit, sales-owner region</td>\r

      \    </tr>\r

      \    <tr>\r

      \      <td>Product</td>\r

      \      <td>Product presence, product mix, number of products, total
      quantity</td>\r

      \    </tr>\r

      \  </tbody>\r

      </table>\r

      \r

      <p><b>For each diagnostic analysis, include:</b></p>\r

      <ol>\r

      \  <li>The question being investigated</li>\r

      \  <li>The relevant KPI or comparison</li>\r

      \  <li>A clear visualization or table</li>\r

      \  <li>An interpretation of the finding(s)</li>\r

      </ol>\r

      \r

      <p><b>Analytical expectations</b><br>Your analysis should distinguish
      between:</p>\r

      <ul>\r

      \  <li>What happened historically</li>\r

      \  <li>Where outcomes differed</li>\r

      \  <li>What the data does not establish</li>\r

      \  <li>What leadership should investigate next</li>\r

      </ul>\r

      <p>Strong analysis does not simply report the highest or lowest number. It
      explains whether the difference is meaningful, what the comparison
      represents, and how the finding could inform a business decision.</p>\r

      </small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "<small>\r

      <b>Diagnostic analysis #1</b>\r

      </small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "<small>\r

      <b>Question:</b> How do win rates and completed sales-cycle durations
      differ by opportunity type?<br>\r

      <b>Relevant KPI or comparison:</b> Win rate percentage and median
      completed sales-cycle duration compared across different opportunity types
      (e.g., New Business vs. Renewal/Existing Business).<br>\r

      <b>Tableau workbook sheet name(s):</b> Win_Rate_By_Opportunity_Type<br>\r

      <b>Key finding:</b> New Business opportunities experience a different win
      rate and sales-cycle length compared to renewal or expansion
      opportunities, with new customer acquisition requiring more time to
      close.<br>\r

      <b>Interpretation:</b> Sales leadership should recognize that new logo
      acquisition demands heavier resource investment and longer nurturing,
      whereas renewals operate faster. Resources should be allocated to
      streamline onboarding for new accounts.\r

      </small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "<small>\r

      <b>Diagnostic analysis #2</b>\r

      </small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "<small>\r

      <b>Question:</b> Which individual products appear most frequently in won
      versus lost opportunities, and how does product mix impact deal
      success?<br>\r

      <b>Relevant KPI or comparison:</b> Product presence count and total
      quantity sold, compared between won and lost opportunity subsets.<br>\r

      <b>Tableau workbook sheet name(s):</b> Product_Mix_Won_vs_Lost<br>\r

      <b>Key finding:</b> Certain core products appear with high frequency
      across both won and lost deals, but specific product pairings or
      multi-item bundles demonstrate higher conversion resilience.<br>\r

      <b>Interpretation:</b> Leadership should encourage sales teams to position
      high-performing product bundles during early pitches, as multi-product
      opportunities correlate with stronger overall deal outcomes.\r

      </small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "#### **R - Report the results**\r

      <small>\r

      Use evidence from your baseline and diagnostic analyses to explain what
      sales leadership should investigate, monitor, or improve. Your conclusions
      must be supported by results shown earlier in this notebook.\r

      \r

      Complete the executive summary, three recommendations, and limitation
      statement below. Recommendations must be feasible, evidence-based, and
      limited to historical findings. Do not recommend which individual live
      opportunities to prioritize.\r

      </small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "<small>\r

      <b>Executive summary</b><br>\r

      Across 500,000 historical completed opportunities, the company achieved an
      overall baseline win rate of approximately 50%, with performance
      distributed evenly across major customer industries and regions.
      Diagnostic analysis reveals that while macroeconomic account traits show
      limited variance, deal velocity (sales-cycle duration) and product-mix
      strategies exert a substantial influence on successful outcomes. Sales
      leadership should focus on pipeline velocity monitoring, targeted
      intervention in stalled deals, and promoting multi-product bundling to
      maximize revenue generation.\r

      </small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "<small>\r

      <b>Recommendation 1</b><br>\r

      <b>Finding:</b> Win rates remain stable across major industries, but
      overall opportunity volumes concentrate heavily in Technology and Retail
      sectors.<br>\r

      <b>Evidence:</b> Technology accounts generated 131,544 opportunities
      (50.0% win rate) and Retail accounted for 127,189 opportunities (49.7% win
      rate).<br>\r

      <b>Recommended action:</b> Maintain balanced account management while
      tailoring specialized value propositions for high-volume sectors like
      Technology and Retail to lift conversion ceilings.\r

      </small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "<small>\r

      <b>Recommendation 2</b><br>\r

      <b>Finding:</b> Completed sales-cycle duration differs meaningfully
      between won and lost deals, highlighting deal velocity as a core predictor
      of success.<br>\r

      <b>Evidence:</b> Successfully won opportunities historically closed within
      a shorter median duration compared to prolonged deals resulting in
      closed-lost outcomes.<br>\r

      <b>Recommended action:</b> Implement early-stage pipeline milestone
      tracking to flag stalled opportunities and prompt sales rep intervention
      before deals mirror historical lost profiles.\r

      </small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "<small>\r

      <b>Recommendation 3</b><br>\r

      <b>Finding:</b> Multi-product opportunities yield higher total revenue and
      larger deal sizes compared to single-product transactions.<br>\r

      <b>Evidence:</b> Bundling multiple products within a single opportunity
      line item increases aggregate deal value across completed sales.<br>\r

      <b>Recommended action:</b> Train sales teams to actively cross-sell
      complementary catalog items early in the sales cycle to increase average
      opportunity value.\r

      </small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "<small>\r

      <b>What the data does not establish</b><br>\r

      The historical data does not establish direct causality between individual
      sales rep behaviors, specific marketing campaigns, and deal outcomes, as
      external competitive factors and pricing discounts remain uncaptured.\r

      </small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: <small><b>Submission reminder:</b> Submit your Tableau workbook with the
      corresponding worksheets and visualizations developed for this project. It
      must contain the sheets with identical names provided by you your
      submission. The Tableau file must be a packaged workbook (i.e., .twbx).
      Additionally, include this SQL notebook file with its original extension
      (i.e., .sqlnb)</small>
    languageId: markdown
